WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.906
- My mom shot my father and--

00:00:02.906 --> 00:00:03.739
- [Interviewer] What happened?

00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:06.160
- She shot my father and
killed him when I was a baby.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.190
And that's why she did what she did

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.893
to me because I'm a spitting image of him.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.410
- [Interviewer] So Benjamin
where are you from?

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.367
- I am from Detroit, Michigan, and--

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:19.200
- [Interviewer] That's my home town.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.033
- Is it really?

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:21.473
I'm from seven mile and Gratiot.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.720
East side.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.930
I moved up there when I was 12

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.970
because the very abusive mother

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.283
and my best friend, his mom adopted me.

00:00:36.255 --> 00:00:37.747
- [Interviewer] I'm sorry,
what happened to your mom?

00:00:37.747 --> 00:00:42.080
- My best friend's mom adopted
me away from my birth mother.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.750
- [Interviewer] Why did
you leave your actual mom?

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.390
- She was, I don't even
want to say abusive,

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.783
she was a torturer, she was ridiculous.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.840
She, psychological, emotional,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.050
physical abuse, sexual abuse,

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.330
anything she could do to
cause my day to be harder

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:05.710
she packaged it up for me
and served it on a cold dish.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.250
It was,

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.630
and when my friend's
mom found out about it

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.670
she was a paralegal and she got her firm

00:01:15.261 --> 00:01:16.760
to take the case and fight it.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.640
It took three years and they

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.753
they got me from them.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:25.553
And I went from like a

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.150
literally a multi multimillionaire family

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.150
to a blue collar, inner city,
black family in Detroit.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.120
It was like the opposite
of the Jeffersons.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.283
You know what I mean?

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.433
It's about a lot of perspective
from both sides of it.

00:01:49.132 --> 00:01:51.930
There's a lot of that yin yang with me.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.942
I have a high IQ, but
a learning disability.

00:01:55.942 --> 00:01:57.030
You know what I mean?

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.200
There's always a freaking
counterbalance or, or a caveat.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.140
You know what I mean?

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.140
It's like, so it's made my
life interestingly difficult,

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.176
but made me appreciate it everything I got

00:02:12.176 --> 00:02:13.980
because I've never had it handed to me.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.270
I've had to struggle a little
harder than the next guy.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.091
And I've gotten to where,
that's the only way I want it.

00:02:21.091 --> 00:02:22.223
- [Interviewer] How old are you?

00:02:22.223 --> 00:02:23.973
- I'll be 44 in June.

00:02:25.214 --> 00:02:27.044
- [Interviewer] Do you
have any kids, girlfriend,

00:02:27.044 --> 00:02:28.311
wife, anything?

00:02:28.311 --> 00:02:31.880
- No, that's where it gets interesting.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.573
I married my best friend growing up.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.620
We knew each other since
we were two years old

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.420
we didn't get married 'til
we were in out twenties

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.287
but we had a daughter in '99.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.123
She's 19 going to my own
alma mater OSU right now.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:59.330
Five years ago, her mother
dropped Lena off at church,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.540
and on her way, home picked
up some White Castles for us.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.820
It was my one day off a week

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:09.820
and was hit head on by a
drunk driver and killed.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.360
He was a fire chief, the
clinic's fire department.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.963
He was on duty and he was schnockered and

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.650
that was the ultimate unfair thing

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.000
because I had it really good.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.850
I had it better than I deserved.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.970
Most people were like,
Figgins, you got a great girl.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.213
You got, you got so lucky.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.260
And I appreciated it.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.380
We finished each other's sentences.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:51.770
We were like that.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.160
I still freaking go to tell her a joke

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.100
and no one else is going to get it.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.150
And it leaves me freaking hollow.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:05.150
And it's, I've got to swallow
it and say, you know what?

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.570
It's part of my path

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.380
but I really think that one was not fair

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:16.380
because I came home and
I was the guy, I worked,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:19.490
I was the breadwinner.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.350
My wife took care of the kid

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.003
took care of the house and the business.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.010
And I tried to juggle it

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.210
for about a week and realized
I cannot raise a daughter,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:38.210
who's 15 years old when I'm
working 14 to 16 hours a day

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.240
including my commute, six days a week,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.360
she's going to be a latchkey
kid, completely unsupervised.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.923
There's nobody nearby to help out.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.550
So not only did I lose my wife

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:56.180
I had to give my daughter to my in laws.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.180
And I lost both my girls
within a week basically

00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:05.433
and my daughter never understood it.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.780
She thinks I abandoned
her and she thinks that

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.780
I ran away to go party and
get high and say, fuck you.

00:05:17.177 --> 00:05:22.020
And I tried to explain to
her a thousand times, Lena,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.540
when you left and your mom wasn't there,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.950
I sat in my house and it was just silent

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.830
and there was nothing.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.240
And it was so depressing--

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.400
- [Interviewer] How far away
were your in laws from you?

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.970
- An hour and a half.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.810
I was in a, if you're familiar with Ohio,

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.660
Athens County down by the Ohio river

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.530
everybody was in Columbus,
including my job.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.413
I drove 90 minutes to two hours
each way to work everyday.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:58.500
And I freaking sunk into the most brutal--

00:05:58.610 --> 00:05:59.670
- [Interviewer] So your job

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.440
and your in-laws were in Columbus,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.490
why didn't you just move to Columbus?

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:07.220
- Because I had recently
inherited my family home

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.570
that had been started by
my great great grandfather

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:12.510
built by hand

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.900
and every generation has
inherited it and added onto it.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.363
And it's, I'm the last,

00:06:21.352 --> 00:06:25.310
and it was important
to me to keep it going.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:29.550
And I couldn't do that with
maintaining another property.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.880
And, you know, we had just,

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.220
we've been established there about a year

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:37.947
and things were good and
I don't know, it just,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.480
the logistics of it
just wouldn't work out.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:47.880
I had, I kind of had to do it.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.720
I was the only one,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.110
it would have fallen
to pieces, if I didn't.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.110
And I, so you know, I, I pretty much,

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.403
in sitting here now,

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:09.410
the last, last of my line
cut off from everybody.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:14.200
And I came to LA

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.650
for a new start because I
didn't know anybody here.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:20.090
And I had no ghosts or demons

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.850
and I can maybe create a fresh slate.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:27.850
And you know what, it's
a curse and a blessing,

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.700
because I've never felt

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.713
I feel even lonelier here.

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:39.250
I rarely have met a couple people.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.390
I can barely call anybody a friend here

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.610
because I use that word very seriously.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.531
It's you know what I mean?

00:07:47.531 --> 00:07:49.643
- [Interviewer] What's the
story with the house now?

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.630
- Well, it kind of involves her,

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.330
around new years I found this dog.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.580
She was unwanted and abused

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.560
and I was on my way to jump
off the fourth street bridge.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:03.900
When I came across her

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.730
she distracted me because I
worked in an animal hospital.

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:13.110
I saw a dog in stress and I took over

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:17.420
and kind of rescued her from
out from under the vehicle.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:20.680
She freaking leaped into my arms and just

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:26.220
she I've never seen a dog
thank somebody so much

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.240
and bond so quickly.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.770
I forgot all about jumping off the bridge

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:35.660
focused on this thing
and said, you know what?

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.370
It's one thing for me to be out here.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.740
It's another thing for a
Chihuahua to be out here.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.740
I'm going to get you

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.670
off the streets sooner rather than later.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.410
And department of mental
health came through recently

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:54.350
and got me in transitional
housing at the Russ Hotel.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:56.540
And I think I'm a month or two away

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.490
from my semipermanent home.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.323
- [Interviewer] Good for you.

00:08:59.323 --> 00:09:02.370
- That'll be like an SRO for a year

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.660
and then that leads to my

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.600
the VA's gonna grant my permanent housing

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.510
which can go anywhere.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.310
- [Interviewer] So do you
get a VA check every month?

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:15.023
- Yeah, it's I only have a 25% disability.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.090
They don't accept my hearing loss.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.047
I drove a tank and most

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.000
every 19 kilo tanker
has some hearing loss.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.260
And now they just came out with

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.520
when we were in Afghanistan,
3m gave us defective,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.473
knowingly gave us defective
hearing aid, hearing protection.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.460
So that just adds to it.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.920
But yeah I'm fighting them again

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.673
and they might play ball with me.

00:09:46.009 --> 00:09:48.823
I just think my best bets just suck it up,

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.250
finish my last semester at school

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.480
and then go back to the workforce

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.283
and just make my own
way, like I always have.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.189
- [Interviewer] So what
happened with the house in Ohio?

00:10:02.189 --> 00:10:03.900
- Well, it was just so depressing there.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.880
I had three or four suicidal attempts

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.560
after my wife and daughter left.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:13.420
I started drinking a lot.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.940
Self-destructing so I just had to leave.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:18.990
I found a friend,

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.313
an old friend of the
family to live nearby.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:27.000
They're being very nice and
going over there and checking

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.330
on it and making sure
everything doesn't go to pot.

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:32.570
- [Interviewer] You still own it?

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:34.283
- Yeah it's still titled to me,

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:37.120
it's out in the middle of nowhere

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.020
and I really don't know if I
could even get much for it out

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:42.370
there because it's, like I said,

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:46.080
with me driving so far,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.240
there's no employment nearby

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.023
it's just so luckily the
property taxes are cheap.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.950
It's somewhat self-sustaining,

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:02.480
it's on the last death throes

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.420
of an old oil well that were
drilled there years ago.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:06.670
It's in coal country.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:11.670
So there's lots of mineral
that kind of pays for like,

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:15.250
it breaks even pretty
much, working scenery,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.313
throwing some solar in there.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:22.885
I don't know we're looking
at if I get some back pay

00:11:22.885 --> 00:11:25.003
from the VA, from this lawsuit,

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:28.370
but I might

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:32.950
I might just depending on what
the VA does in my settlement

00:11:34.513 --> 00:11:38.300
kick it here until everything
gets settled with them.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:41.833
And if I'm, if they're going
to go ahead and retire me,

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.030
I might just go there and retire.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:49.430
And just, I think my
attitude's a lot healthier now,

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.690
and I would set myself up for some bumper

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:59.120
so I didn't go down like
that again, you know,

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.330
but I'd like to return there.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:03.820
I love the peace and quiet

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:08.287
and the nature and it's good for me.

00:12:08.287 --> 00:12:13.287
And I think without such
a horrible situation

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:14.760
I think I can embrace it

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:19.240
and give it a good honest shot.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:22.997
I just like to find someone
to share it with, that's all.

00:12:22.997 --> 00:12:23.830
- [Interviewer] Good for you.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:24.830
- Thank you.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.480
- [Interviewer] How are
you doing now with alcohol

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.010
and you're in the middle
of drug central here,

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:30.843
are you--

00:12:30.843 --> 00:12:34.150
- I actually, and part of the deal,

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:38.310
with getting the housing is
this is housing for healthcare

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:41.160
kind of the triple H initiatives

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.173
that were passed two years ago,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:51.040
it requires mental
health care, connections,

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:54.450
medical care connections,
primary care doctor,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:55.530
regular visits,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:13:00.070
linked up with them and
drug and alcohol counseling

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.090
and an active participation
in the program.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:04.580
So right now I am

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:09.580
in twice a week intensive
outpatient drug program

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:11.480
at homeless healthcare

00:13:13.058 --> 00:13:15.400
for the substance abuse,
a great caseworker.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:16.683
They're a great team.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.113
- [Interviewer] So what,
what drugs were you using?

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.610
- I was always opiates and marijuana

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:30.870
but that's I'm on like,

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.030
they call it medicated,
medically assisted therapy.

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.130
So I take Suboxone every day.

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:42.130
It's kind of a, kind of like
an abuses for alcoholics.

00:13:42.429 --> 00:13:43.840
And it takes the withdraws away

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.420
prevents you from like
feeling any if you did use.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.752
So it makes it futile.

00:13:49.752 --> 00:13:51.100
You know what I mean?

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.940
It's pointless to use.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:57.790
And it gives you that it
gives you what you don't have

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:01.730
at first and at the
early recovery, you know,

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:06.663
which is a Bulletproof vest.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.830
So I can get my patterns established.

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:14.150
My connections established,
my routine established,

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.270
without having to worry

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:20.270
about kicking, physically and mentally,

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:25.593
one of the toughest
substances there is to kick.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:31.000
It's something that's a hell
of a lot easier to wean off of.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:32.420
So if I ever do want to move

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.863
off of it later, that's an option.

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:37.930
But right now everybody agrees.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:39.250
This is the best thing.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:44.250
And it seems to be working,
I'm giving them clean urines.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:49.380
And that just something stupid

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:50.970
like that just makes me happy.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:54.090
Like if it's something
I accomplished, I did,

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:59.090
and it was like, I'm attaining my goals,

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.280
and that's something I
haven't done in five years.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.010
And then it makes me feel good.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:05.990
And I think I'm scratching

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:09.200
and clawing my way back to
some sort of freaking light.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.913
'Cause this almost got me,

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:18.530
the homeless thing almost got me to where

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:23.530
I was just this angry
muttering, spiteful, antisocial,

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:32.550
fucking this societal norms non-conformist

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:40.830
and just unwilling to participate
in society on any level.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.360
And God let a little bit

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.850
of sunshine in and I just ran with it.

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:49.820
And I think I'm going to be much happier

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:54.820
if I build my life back
instead of just say it's over

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:58.163
and just let it go down the toilet.

00:15:59.485 --> 00:16:01.135
- [Interviewer] That's excellent.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:06.110
- [Benjamin] I've got over
a dozen suicide attempts,

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:08.800
including guns to my head.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.600
I'm still here.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:13.470
And there's no reason I should be.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.428
So I've come to accept

00:16:14.428 --> 00:16:18.183
that it's not my time.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:21.000
There's a reason I'm not dead

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:27.990
because I had there's no

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.140
there's no explanation for it.

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:32.899
I absolutely, doctors are baffled,

00:16:32.899 --> 00:16:34.720
my family's baffled,

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:36.580
anybody around is just like

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.250
how the heck did you get through that?

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:44.250
It's because there's
something on this earth that

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:46.970
I haven't done yet.

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:48.163
It's bigger than me.

00:16:49.379 --> 00:16:51.029
I don't know what the hell it is.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:00.690
Some sort of entity in the
universe has tasked me with

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:09.410
carrying some sort of
responsibility to this world.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:14.420
And I think it's possibly
teaching somebody something

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:17.300
or possibly mentoring somebody.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:18.650
So they don't have to take some

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:23.650
of the bumps and bruises I
did, but who the hell knows?

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.880
And I've sort of just said, okay.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:33.833
And I've taken an approach
where I'm not actively

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.980
seeking it but keeping half an eye out

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:42.980
for those breadcrumb trails
of, okay, I think I get it now.

00:17:44.088 --> 00:17:46.507
- [Interviewer] I think
you're onto something.

00:17:46.507 --> 00:17:48.963
- My mom shot my father and--

00:17:48.963 --> 00:17:49.796
- [Interviewer] What happened?

00:17:49.796 --> 00:17:52.240
- She shot my father and
killed him when I was a baby.

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:54.100
And that's why she did what she did to me

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:56.963
because I'm a spitting image of him.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:01.280
So I never met my father really

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.540
because I was an infant when he passed

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.360
I walk like him, talk like
him, same sense of humor,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:09.660
and my mom having that guilt

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:12.533
she just used to be like--

00:18:12.533 --> 00:18:14.760
- [Interviewer] She never got caught?

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:19.570
- Yeah, but let's put it this way,

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:23.150
this happened in the middle
of nowhere in the house

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:24.063
they just built.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:29.990
Nobody knows what happened,
but her and my dad, and me,

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:32.300
but I was a year and a half old.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:33.700
We were the only ones there.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:37.190
My mother, she moved the body.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.330
There was enough confusion

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:50.270
at the scene to where it wasn't,

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.150
it was the late seventies, you know,

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.640
the crime solving technology

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:57.113
wasn't what it is now.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:02.010
And it looked like a suicide
and they didn't want to

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:06.260
you know as suicides that they
go with kid gloves and they,

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:09.830
they just kind of, okay,
and they left it at that.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:14.293
And years later, I just
wouldn't give up on it.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:18.090
And I dug at my grandmother and I said,

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.280
what the hell happened?

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.130
And she said, you know what happened.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:27.400
She said, we covered for your mother.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:32.843
Didn't want a tragedy to turn into two.

00:19:35.542 --> 00:19:37.360
You're tire and rubber.

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:39.230
That's my grandfather.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.760
Like he was president
of Goodyear and Allchin,

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.890
Blue blood, old money.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:48.307
They didn't want a family
newspaper headline story.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.640
And you know, it was swept under the rug.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.200
That's how things were handled back then.

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:02.200
And when I pushed my grandmother,
she finally came clean,

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.553
and I pushed it.

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:09.150
- [Interviewer] Your
Grandmother being your dads mom?

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:10.070
- My mom's mom.

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:10.903
- [Interviewer] Your mom's mom?

00:20:10.903 --> 00:20:14.473
- Yeah I,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.720
it got as far as the prosecutor

00:20:21.898 --> 00:20:25.930
the district attorney, looked at it,

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:30.930
took a really honest stab at a case

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.530
and just said, Benjamin,

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:34.753
there's too much hearsay.

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:39.070
You know how eye witness
accounts are anyway

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:41.943
and these are 40 years old and it's just,

00:20:41.943 --> 00:20:45.710
I'm not going to win
this with a jury trial.

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:48.690
It's going to drag your whole
family through the mud anyway.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.632
And you know what she won.

00:20:51.632 --> 00:20:55.090
I'm sorry to say, I know what happened.

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:59.900
You know it happened, but we
can't get a case out of it.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:01.587
So that's where it stands.

00:21:04.277 --> 00:21:07.960
But you know, I'm just
glad I know what's up.

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:12.960
And like I said, my mother's
miserable, she's miserable.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.010
She's paying for it.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:18.163
- [Interviewer] How old is she now?

00:21:19.143 --> 00:21:21.420
- Born in 52.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:23.703
So yeah, she's in her late sixties.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.297
She would have to--

00:21:28.199 --> 00:21:29.539
- [Interviewer] So 66, 67--

00:21:29.539 --> 00:21:32.610
- I think she's 67 in September.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:37.093
Yeah she's still around.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:42.550
I guess my daughter went and I told my kid

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:46.593
she happens to be living
three blocks away from her.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.720
I said please don't be tempted to go

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:50.950
over to that lady's house.

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:53.350
I know you don't want to grow up

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:55.480
without your grandmother in your life.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:56.810
That's not a grandmother.

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:59.683
That's something you
don't want to play with.

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:02.410
And Lena didn't get it.

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:07.240
And when I was unable to be
reached out here, off the grid,

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:10.890
on skid row, she went to my
mom and like, hey, where's Ben?

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:14.130
My dad is out of communication with me.

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:15.700
I need to know where he's at.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:17.860
Do you have any information?

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:21.573
She told her I died of
drug abuse years ago.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.553
And Lena mourned me and cried.

00:22:27.602 --> 00:22:29.290
And that's her blood man.

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:30.470
That's her granddaughter.

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:33.690
And she made her cry and
thought nothing of it.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:38.330
And when Lena got ahold of me again,

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:40.400
I said, I hate to say I tell you so dude,

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.620
but you're petting a snake

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:44.463
and you're going to be a bit
every time with Gail Hinshaw.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:49.174
And she said okay dad, I cried uncle.

00:22:49.174 --> 00:22:52.053
And I said, here's an email account.

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:54.234
I don't care what happens.

00:22:54.234 --> 00:22:59.234
You can always find me
with this no matter what.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.020
And she feels better with that now.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:03.763
So no matter where we're in touch,

00:23:05.196 --> 00:23:07.927
but we're mending a lot of broken pieces

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:15.920
of our relationship and it's
slow going, but it's there.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:19.790
And I hope to see it come
all the way back one day.

00:23:21.514 --> 00:23:26.514
But if by some chance
I'm able to be redeemed

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:36.590
at the end of my life here
on the last half of it

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:39.730
it would be so cool

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:44.723
because I've never gotten
a chance to do that.

00:23:45.572 --> 00:23:47.150
I've never gotten

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:51.820
at the end to be smiling,
feeling satisfied.

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:54.420
I always bust my ass

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:55.690
worked to the top

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:57.850
and then get kicked
back down to the bottom

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:00.274
like Sisyphus and his rock.

00:24:00.274 --> 00:24:02.210
And I'd like to

00:24:05.344 --> 00:24:07.620
I think this is going to be my one shot

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:12.250
to hold the trophy up and say I did it.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:15.550
I'm going to say I'm not going
to waste that opportunity

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:20.130
because they're obviously rare in my world

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.040
and I want to run with it.

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:23.050
- [Interviewer] Good for you.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:24.030
- So I like to say we all

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:27.903
haven't seen the last of
me, big things coming.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:30.520
- [Interviewer] Thank you so
much for sharing your story.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:31.470
- You're very well.

